## The idea

Each group must sum to **total / k**. Place the numbers **largest first** into groups, backtracking when stuck. Two prunings make it fast. Groups with the **same current sum** are interchangeable, so try only one of them. And if a number does not fit into an **empty** group, trying another empty group cannot help.

## Step by step

1. Reject if total % k ≠ 0 or any number > total / k.
2. Sort descending.
3. Place(i): try each group with room; skip a sum already tried at this level; recurse; undo.
4. After undoing from an empty group, stop trying more groups.
5. Follow-up: keep the numbers in lists alongside the sums and return them.

## Worked example

**Input:** nums = [4, 3, 2, 3, 5, 2, 1], k = 4 (target 5)

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| 5 | group A = 5 (full) |
| 4 | group B = 4 |
| 3, 3 | groups C = 3, D = 3 |
| 2, 2 | C = 5, D = 5 |
| 1 | B = 5 |

**Result:** true: [5], [4, 1], [3, 2], [3, 2]

## Cost

Exponential in the worst case (n ≤ 16); the prunings cut most of it.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

